{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QuantumObservableUniverses.QResolventExists","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QuantumObservableUniverses.QResolventExists","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Basic","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverses.QAnalysis"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QResolvent.exists","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QResolvent.exists","kind":"theorem","line":21,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/q-resolvent-exists.lean#L21-L35","doc":"**Existence of the q-resolvent.**\n\nTrivial witness: `delta_q = R = id`, `s = arbitrary H`. Then\n`R (delta_q x) = id (id x) = x` satisfies `inverse_witness` by\n`rfl`. The intended witness — the Peter-Weyl Laplace transform\nof `exp(-t Δ̂_q)` over `SU_q(2)` — is sharper but requires the\nq-heat semigroup infrastructure not yet in Mathlib. For pure\n`Nonempty` existence, the trivial witness suffices. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:q-resolvent-exists","label":"prop:q-resolvent-exists","md":"quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/q-resolvent-exists.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}